---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/indas/indas29.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Identifying a Hyperinflationary Economy" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Restatement of Historical Cost Financial Statements" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Monetary vs Non-Monetary Items" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Price Index & Gain/Loss on Net Monetary Position" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Application for Indian Groups with Foreign Operations" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Corresponding Figures" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Ind AS 29 vs IAS 29 — Full Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical restatement philosophy to IAS 29</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 29 applies the IDENTICAL principle as IAS 29 — in a HYPERINFLATIONARY economy, reporting operating results/financial position in the LOCAL currency WITHOUT restatement is NOT meaningful, since money loses purchasing power at such a rate that comparing amounts from different time periods is misleading even within a single period.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IND AS 29 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list"><span><strong>Relevance</strong> — restating financial statements to a CURRENT purchasing power basis using a general price index — identical to IAS 29</span><span><strong>Narrow but genuine Indian relevance</strong> — the RUPEE itself has never met the hyperinflationary threshold, so Ind AS 29 does NOT apply to Indian standalone operations — but Indian MULTINATIONAL groups with subsidiaries/branches in hyperinflationary jurisdictions must apply it when CONSOLIDATING those foreign operations</span></div>
        <div class="note">This is NOT a divergence in Ind AS 29's own principles from IAS 29 — it is simply a standard with LIMITED domestic Indian applicability, relevant only through foreign operation consolidation.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">The core restatement formula — identical to IAS 29</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><div class="f-eq">Restated amount = Historical cost amount × (Price index at reporting date ÷ Price index at date of transaction)</div></div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">IDENTICAL restatement mechanism to IAS 29.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw"><div class="ctitle cw">What Ind AS 29 covers</div><div class="principle">Ind AS 29 applies to the financial statements of ANY entity whose FUNCTIONAL currency is that of a hyperinflationary economy — IDENTICAL scope to IAS 29.</div><div class="note">For Indian companies, this is relevant ONLY when consolidating a foreign operation whose functional currency meets the hyperinflationary threshold.</div></div>
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Hyperinflationary economy</div><div class="glossary-def">IDENTICAL definition to IAS 29 — characteristics include cumulative inflation over three years approaching or exceeding 100%, among other indicators (no single mechanical threshold).</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">General price index</div><div class="glossary-def">A published index reflecting changes in general purchasing power, used for restatement — identical concept to IAS 29.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Identifying a Hyperinflationary Economy (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical qualitative indicators to IAS 29</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example 1: An Indian group's foreign subsidiary</div><div class="wk-body">An Indian conglomerate has a manufacturing subsidiary in a country where cumulative three-year inflation has approached 100%, the general population prefers to hold wealth in non-monetary assets or a relatively stable foreign currency, and prices are commonly quoted in that foreign currency.</div><div class="wk-answer">This subsidiary's economy likely meets IAS 29's/Ind AS 29's IDENTICAL qualitative indicators for hyperinflation, requiring the subsidiary's financial statements to be RESTATED to current purchasing power BEFORE consolidation into the Indian parent's Ind AS financial statements.</div></div>
    </div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Restatement of Historical Cost Financial Statements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Identical to IAS 29</div><div class="text-supporting">Non-monetary items are restated using a general price index; monetary items are NOT restated (they are already expressed in current purchasing power terms) — IDENTICAL to IAS 29.</div></div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Monetary vs Non-Monetary Items</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical distinction to IAS 29</div><div class="text-supporting">The SAME monetary/non-monetary distinction used in Ind AS 21 applies here — monetary items (cash, receivables, payables) are NOT restated; non-monetary items (PPE, inventories, equity) ARE restated — IDENTICAL to IAS 29.</div></div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Price Index &amp; Gain/Loss on Net Monetary Position</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical to IAS 29</div><div class="text-supporting">A gain/loss on the NET MONETARY POSITION arises from holding monetary assets/liabilities during a period of inflation, recognised in P&amp;L — IDENTICAL to IAS 29, reflecting the purchasing power impact of holding monetary items.</div></div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Application for Indian Groups with Foreign Operations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The single most important practical Indian consideration</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Indian MULTINATIONAL groups with operations in historically hyperinflationary jurisdictions must apply Ind AS 29 to RESTATE that foreign operation's financial statements BEFORE translation and consolidation under Ind AS 21/110 — a two-step process (restate for hyperinflation, THEN translate to the Indian parent's presentation currency) IDENTICAL to how IAS 29/IAS 21 interact for any global group with comparable foreign operations.</div>
    </div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Corresponding Figures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Identical to IAS 29</div><div class="text-supporting">Comparative figures are ALSO restated to the CURRENT reporting period's measuring unit, so ALL amounts are expressed in a consistent current purchasing power basis — IDENTICAL to IAS 29.</div></div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical disclosure requirements to IAS 29</div><div class="check-list"><span>Fact that financial statements have been restated for changes in general purchasing power</span><span>Identity and level of the price index used</span><span>Gain/loss on net monetary position</span></div></div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a foreign operation's economy meets the hyperinflationary threshold (a qualitative, multi-factor assessment)</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Selection of an appropriate general price index where multiple indices exist for a jurisdiction</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">Indian groups should MONITOR foreign operation jurisdictions for changes in hyperinflationary status, since this determination can CHANGE over time as economic conditions evolve.</div>
    </div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Ind AS 29 vs IAS 29 — Full Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comprehensive comparison table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IAS 29</span><span>Ind AS 29</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Restatement methodology, hyperinflation indicators</span><span>Standard model</span><span>IDENTICAL — no substantive divergence</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Domestic applicability</span><span>Applies wherever a jurisdiction meets the threshold</span><span>NEVER applicable to Indian Rupee-functional-currency operations — relevant only via foreign operation consolidation</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Ind AS 29 is essentially IDENTICAL to IAS 29 in substance — its Indian relevance is NARROW (foreign operation consolidation only), similar in character to how any IFRS-reporting group without domestic hyperinflation would apply IAS 29.</div>
    </div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every Ind AS 29 adjustment lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Item</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Restated non-monetary items</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Restated before translation/consolidation</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Gain/loss on net monetary position</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Identical to IAS 29</span></div>
      </div>
    </div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 29</span><span>The global counterpart standard, near-identically converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 21</span><span>Governs the subsequent translation of a hyperinflation-restated foreign operation</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 110</span><span>Governs consolidation of the foreign operation into the Indian parent's group financial statements</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Net monetary position</div><div class="glossary-def">The excess of monetary assets over monetary liabilities (or vice versa), generating a purchasing power gain/loss during inflation.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Measuring unit current at the balance sheet date</div><div class="glossary-def">The current purchasing power basis to which all restated amounts, including comparatives, are expressed.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Hyperinflationary economy", definition: "IDENTICAL definition to IAS 29 — characteristics include cumulative inflation over three years approaching or exceeding 100%, among other indicators (no single mechanical threshold)." },
      { term: "General price index", definition: "A published index reflecting changes in general purchasing power, used for restatement — identical concept to IAS 29." },
      { term: "Net monetary position", definition: "The excess of monetary assets over monetary liabilities (or vice versa), generating a purchasing power gain/loss during inflation." },
      { term: "Measuring unit current at the balance sheet date", definition: "The current purchasing power basis to which all restated amounts, including comparatives, are expressed." },
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
